from ../meaning/Relationships import Relationships
from ./OpenItems import OpenItems
from ./DetectionRules import DetectionRules

export anchor SemanticModel:
    description:
        Facts on their own are a list. The semantic model connects them into
        a graph: a set of things, and the links between them. It is the
        single result of analysis that everything else is computed from:
        measures, open items, and instructions.

    whyAGraph:
        A specification is a web, not a list. Definitions refer to other
        definitions. Conditions limit facts. States change behavior. Anchors
        inherit from anchors. A requirement depends on the things it
        mentions being defined. Questions such as what depends on
        Button.action, or which of Button's facts only hold when it is
        disabled, are questions about links, and a graph answers them
        directly.

    nodes:
        ```text
        entity       a top-level declaration                Button
        part         a property or introduced part          Button.action
        value        a value a fact gives                   blue
        fact         one fact                               description/s3/f1
        condition    one condition                          Button.state = disabled
        statement    one statement of source text           Button.disabled/s3
        open item    one gap, ambiguity, contradiction,
                     or unrecognized statement              Button.action is never defined
        ```

    edges:
        Every edge has one of the relationship kinds in @{Relationships}.
        Some come straight from structure, such as Defines and Inherits. Some
        come from facts, such as Invokes. And some record how the model was
        built: StatedIn links a fact to its statement, DerivedFrom links a
        derived fact to its inputs, and Conditions links a condition to the
        facts it limits. Every open item is linked to the facts and
        statements it is about.

    example:
        Part of the running example's graph.

        ```text
        Button --Defines--> Button.disabled
        Button --Contains--> Button.action          (introduced)
        Button --Contains--> Button.text            (introduced)

        fact "on Button clicked: invoke Button.action"
            --StatedIn--> Button.description/s2
            Button --Invokes--> Button.action

        condition "Button.state = disabled"
            --Conditions--> fact "Button cannot be clicked"
            --Conditions--> fact "Button.color = dim(Button.color)"
            --Conditions--> fact "Button.text = dim(Button.text)"

        open item "Button.action is used but never defined"
            --> Button.action, fact "on Button clicked: invoke Button.action"
        ```

    oneModel:
        One analysis run builds one model over every analyzed file, so links
        can cross files: a fact in one file can depend on a part defined in
        another. Views that show part of it, such as one entity, are cut from
        this single model rather than built separately.

    openItems: @{OpenItems}
    detectionRules: @{DetectionRules}
